When the rafts returned upstream, the first elder rejoiced. Both villages had more than when they started. This exchange became tradition, repeated each season.

Some seasons, storms obscured the rafts' contents, causing confusion. Yet both villages learned that occasional misunderstandings were merely noise in the river's flow. What mattered was the underlying current of reciprocal trust.

